      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p
          class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
        >
          Most treadmill content on the internet is organized like a
          reference manual: a section on motors, a section on decks, a
          section on price. That's useful information, but it isn't how
          buying actually happens. Buying a treadmill is a sequence of
          decisions, each one narrowing the field before the next, and
          getting the order wrong is exactly how people end up with a machine
          that's technically excellent and practically wrong for their home.
          This guide walks through that sequence in the order we'd actually
          recommend making each call, from the first decision — your budget —
          through the last one — how you maintain the machine years down the
          road. If you want the deeper reference breakdown of motor power,
          continuous horsepower ratings, and deck cushioning instead, our
          companion piece, the
          <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026"
            >Complete Home Treadmill Buying Guide</a
          >, covers that ground in detail. This page is the checklist you
          work through alongside it.
        </p>

        <!-- STEP 1 -->
        <h2 id="step-1" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Step 1: Set Your Budget Range First
        </h2>
        <p>
          Before you look at a single model name, decide what you're willing
          to spend — as a range, not a single number. This sounds obvious,
          but skipping it is the single most common reason people end up
          overwhelmed by treadmill shopping. Without a budget range narrowing
          the field first, you're comparing hundreds of machines spanning
          $300 to $4,000 against each other on features alone, which makes
          every listing look either suspiciously cheap or unreasonably
          expensive depending on what you happened to click on last.
        </p>
        <p>
          Setting the range first flips that. Once you know you're shopping
          between, say, $700 and $1,200, you're only ever comparing machines
          against realistic peers, and decisions about which specific model
          to buy get dramatically easier. We split our own coverage into
          budget tiers for exactly this reason, so once you've settled on a
          range, go straight to the roundup that matches it rather than
          browsing everything:
        </p>
        <p>
          If your ceiling is under $1,500, our
          <a href="/treadmills-under-1500">treadmills under $1,500 guide</a>
          covers the sweet spot where most households land — enough budget for
          a genuinely sturdy frame without paying for commercial-grade
          overkill. If you're working with an even tighter number, our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews"
            >treadmills under $1,000 guide</a
          > narrows further to the best value picks below four figures. And if
          you've decided this is a long-term investment and you're comfortable
          spending more to get commercial-grade durability, our
          <a href="/treadmills-over-2500">treadmills over $2,500 guide</a> covers
          the higher end, where frame rigidity, motor headroom, and console quality
          all step up noticeably.
        </p>
        <p>
          One practical tip on setting the number itself: budget for the
          treadmill you'll actually use in year three, not the one that looks
          adequate on day one. A machine bought at the very bottom of your
          comfortable range often feels fine during the first few weeks of
          motivated new-equipment enthusiasm, then starts to feel
          underpowered once your pace, frequency, or household usage
          increases. Padding your range by even $100-$150 above your initial
          instinct tends to buy meaningfully more durability at the margin,
          since that's often the gap between an entry-level motor and deck
          and a genuinely mid-range one.
        </p>

        <!-- STEP 2 -->
        <h2 id="step-2" class="scroll-mt-24">Step 2: Measure Your Space</h2>
        <p>
          With a budget range set, the next step isn't browsing models — it's
          grabbing a tape measure. This step gets skipped constantly because
          it feels less exciting than looking at treadmills, but doing it now
          rather than after you've already fallen for a specific model saves
          an enormous amount of frustration later.
        </p>
        <h3>Room dimensions</h3>
        <p>
          Measure the exact footprint where the treadmill will live during
          use, not just where you think there's roughly enough room. Most
          home treadmills need a used footprint of about 3 feet by 7 feet at
          minimum, and safety guidelines generally recommend at least 2 feet
          of clearance on both sides and behind the machine — behind
          especially, since that's the space your body needs if you were to
          step or stumble off the back of a moving belt. Measure wall to
          wall, then subtract for any furniture, doorways that swing inward,
          or radiators and vents that eat into usable floor space.
        </p>
        <h3>Ceiling height</h3>
        <p>
          This is the measurement people forget most often, and it matters
          for two different reasons depending on what you're buying. First,
          for anyone tall or anyone who wants to use the incline function,
          confirm you have enough vertical clearance above the console at
          maximum incline — a treadmill inclined to 12-15% raises the front
          of the deck and console noticeably higher than it sits flat.
          Second, if you're considering a folding treadmill, measure the
          ceiling height at the exact spot where it will stand folded
          upright, since a folded treadmill can stand 5.5 to 6 feet tall, and
          a sloped basement ceiling or a low light fixture can turn out to be
          a dealbreaker you only discover after delivery.
        </p>
        <h3>Doorway and hallway width for delivery</h3>
        <p>
          Even if the assembled treadmill fits your workout room perfectly,
          it still has to physically get there in its crate, and that's a
          separate measurement problem. Treadmill delivery boxes are large —
          often 7 to 8 feet long — and while delivery crews typically tip the
          box onto its edge to clear standard doorways, tight hallway turns,
          narrow basement stairwells, or an especially tight 90-degree corner
          partway through the path can stop a delivery cold. Walk the actual
          delivery route from your front door to the final room, measuring
          the tightest point along the way, not just the destination doorway.
          If your path includes a stairwell turn or an unusually narrow hall,
          it's worth calling the retailer's delivery line before you buy to
          ask about their experience with tight spaces — most delivery
          companies have seen this exact problem before and can tell you
          what's realistic.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write these three numbers down — usable floor footprint, ceiling
          clearance, and delivery path width — before you move to the next
          step. They'll rule out entire categories of machine faster than any
          amount of feature comparison will.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                3x7 ft
              </p>
