      <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 my-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Should I buy from a showroom or order online?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Ordering online is fine and usually cheaper, but if a showroom
              is within reasonable driving distance, use it as a research
              stop rather than a purchase decision. Walk or jog on two or
              three machines in your budget range to calibrate what "quiet"
              and "smooth" actually feel like in person, then buy whichever
              specific model fits your space and price best, even if that
              means ordering it online afterward.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How do I know if a treadmill will actually fit through my
              doorway?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Check the manufacturer's listed crated dimensions, not the
              assembled footprint, since the box is what has to travel
              through your doorways and hallways during delivery. Most
              standard doorways are <strong>30-32 inches</strong> wide, and most
              treadmill crates fit that on their edge, but narrow stairwells,
              tight hallway turns, and low basement doorframes are where
              deliveries actually get stuck. Measure the tightest point in
              the path, not just the destination room.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What does "starting at" pricing actually mean on a treadmill
              listing?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              It almost always means the price shown is for the base
              configuration without the console, incline range, or
              accessories shown in the product photos. Treadmill retailers
              commonly advertise a stripped-down SKU's price while
              displaying images of a higher trim level. Always click through
              to the specific configuration you're planning to buy and
              confirm the price updates to match before treating any
              headline number as real.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Is it normal for a new treadmill belt to need adjustment in the
              first month?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Belts stretch slightly during their first several weeks of
              use as the material seats into its running position, and a
              belt that felt properly tensioned on delivery day often needs
              a quarter-turn tightening of the rear roller bolts a few weeks
              in. This is routine maintenance, not a defect, and it's worth
              checking belt tension and centering around the two-week and
              six-week marks specifically.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How long is a typical treadmill return window, and does
              assembly void it?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Return windows commonly range from <strong
                >30 to 90 days</strong
              > depending on the retailer, and most explicitly allow assembly
              without voiding the return, since you generally can't evaluate a
              treadmill without putting it together and running on it. What does
              commonly void or complicate a return is disposing of the original
              packaging too early, so hold onto the crate and packing materials
              until you're past the return window and confident in the machine.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do I need to register my treadmill's warranty separately, or is
              my receipt enough?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Keep your receipt, but also register the unit directly with
              the manufacturer, since most brands require an active
              registration on file to process a warranty claim quickly
              rather than relying on a retailer receipt alone. Registration
              typically takes a few minutes online using the serial number
              found on the frame near the motor housing, and doing it during
              your first week of ownership means you won't be scrambling to
              find records months later if something needs a claim.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
